    <section id="rules" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">01</p>
      <h2>The rules</h2>
      <p>Canadian Highlander (Canlander for short) is a two-player constructed format. You build a deck from nearly any Magic card ever printed, and you play it against one opponent, starting at 20 life, with normal Magic rules.</p>
      <dl class="rules-table">
        <div><dt>Deck size</dt><dd>At least 100 cards. There's no maximum, but almost everyone plays exactly 100.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Copies</dt><dd>One of each card. Basic lands, and cards that explicitly allow any number (like Relentless Rats), are the exceptions.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Sideboard</dt><dd>None. That also means companions don't work as companions.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Points</dt><dd>Your deck may contain no more than 10 points' worth of cards from the <a href="{{ site.official.points }}">points list</a>.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Banned</dt><dd>The <a href="https://magic.wizards.com/en/banned-restricted-list">Vintage banned list</a>, plus a handful of extra categories: ante cards, Conspiracies, cards with stickers or attractions, dexterity cards, Acorn and silver-bordered cards, sub-game cards, and cards Wizards has removed for racist depictions.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Restricted</dt><dd>Doesn't matter. Everything is one-of already.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="aside-note">The Canlander Council maintains the official rules at <a href="{{ site.official.rules }}">canadianhighlander.ca</a>. This page is a plain-English summary; theirs is the one that counts.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="points" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">02</p>
      <h2>How points work</h2>
      <p>Most formats deal with overpowered cards by banning them. Canlander doesn't. Instead, a few dozen of the format's strongest cards have a point value, somewhere from 1 to 7 or more. Every other card in Magic is worth zero.</p>
      <p>You get ten points per deck. You can spend them however you like: one huge card, a handful of small ones, or none at all. A deck with zero points is completely legal and often competitive.</p>
      <p>The council revisits the list a few times a year. Cards get added when they start warping the format, get bumped up when they're still too strong, and occasionally come off the list when they've been tamed.</p>

